                %li
                  %a{:href => "#concrete_smooth_sets_diffeological_spaces"} Concrete smooth sets: Diffeological spaces
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#DifferentialForms"} Differential forms
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#integration_and_transgression"} Integration and transgression
          %h2#CoordinateSystemsLayerMod Abstract coordinate systems
          %p
            As discussed in the chapter
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+categories+and+toposes"} categories and toposes
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            is modeled on a collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generator"} archetypical
            basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
            and geometric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphisms"} homomorphisms
            between them. In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            the archetypical spaces are the abstract standard
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian coordinate systems
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            (in every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{n_1} \to \mathbb{R}^{n_2}
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+transformations"} coordinate transformations
          %p
            Here we introduce the basic concept, organizing them in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+spaces"} Cartesian spaces
            (Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CartSpCategory"}
            below.) We highlight three classical theorems about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            in Prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AlgebraicFactsOfDifferentialGeometry"}
            below, which look innocent but play a decisive role in setting up
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+differential+geometry"} synthetic differential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergeometry"} supergeometry
            based on the concept of abstract smooth coordinate systems.
          %p
            At this point these are not yet coordinate systems
            %em on
            some other space. But by applying the general machine of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+categories+and+toposes"} categories and toposes
            to these, a concept of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+spaces"} generalized spaces
            modeled on these abstract coordinate systems is induced. These are the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
            discussed in the next chapter
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+sets"} geometry of physics – smooth sets
          %h3#TheContinuumRealWorldLine The continuum real line
          %p
            The fundamental premise of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            as a model of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is the following.
          %p
            %strong Premise.
            %em
              The abstract
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
              of any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
              is modeled by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          %p This comes down to the following sequence of premises.
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                There is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+ordering"} linear ordering
                of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/points"} points
                on a
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℤ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
                \.
            %li
              %p
                These intervals may each be subdivided into
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                smaller intervals, for each natural number
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
                in an order-preserving way by the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℚ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
                \.
            %li
              %p
                This labeling is dense: every point on the worldline is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supremum"} supremum
                of an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inhabited+set"} inhabited
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bounded+subset"} bounded subset
                of such labels. This means that a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldline"} worldline
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} is
                the
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
                \.
          %p
            The adjective “real” in “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"}> real number
            ” is a historical shadow of the old idea that real numbers are related to
            = succeed "," do
              %em observed reality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            in this way. The experimental success of this assumption shows that it is valid at least to very good approximation.
          %h3#CartesianSpaces Cartesian spaces and smooth functions
          #SmoothFunctions.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"}> smooth function
                )
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
              is called a
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
              if,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coinduction"} coinductively
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative"} derivative
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %mi d
                            %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mi d
                            %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mo →
                        %mi ℝ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{d f}{d x} : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}
                  exists;
              %li
                %p and is itself a smooth function.
            %p
              We write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(\mathbb{R}) \in Set
              for the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              of all smooth functions on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The superscript “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}^\infty
              ” in “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(\mathbb{R})
              ” refers to the order of the
